lization to happen  sooner before a new instance serves live  traffic  you can do this by using a warm-up  request which loads application code  into an instance ahead of time  before any live requests reach it app  engine attempts to detect when your app  needs a new  instance and initiates a warm-up request  to initialize it  any new instances accept requests after  they finish loading your app's code  new requests can then be handled faster  warm-up requests are used by the app  engine scheduler which controls  auto-scaling of instances based on your  configuration  app engine issues get requests to ah  warm up  and then you can implement handlers for  this request  in your code to perform application  specific tasks  like pre-caching app data for most  supported languages  just add the warm-up element under the  inbound services directive  in your app.yaml file then create a  handler that will process  requests that are sent to ah.warmup your  handler should perform  any warm-up logic that is needed by your  app  let's walk through a go example our  function performs the required setup  steps for the application to function  and it logs when an app engine warm-up  request is used to create the new  instance  these warm-up steps happen in setup for  consistency with cold start  instances everything else remains the  same  this setup function executes the  per-instance one-time warm-up  and initialization actions finally  the index handler responds to requests  with our greeting  one thing to note is warm-up requests  don't work in all cases  despite the best effort attempt to send  requests to already warmed up instances  when enabled so you might still face  loading requests like if the instance is  the first one being started up  or if there's a steep ramp up in traffic  in these cases  you should use the resident instances  which you can learn about below  once warm-up requests were implemented  critter junction was able to reduce cold  start instances during 